Overview

This lab focused on enrolling and managing a Windows 11 device using Microsoft Intune.

The objective was to understand how an IT administrator can bring a Windows endpoint under cloud-based device management and verify its management status from the Intune admin center.

---

## Lab Environment

### Microsoft 365 / Intune

- Microsoft 365 tenant: CharlyCyber

- Microsoft Intune Plan 1 Trial

- Intune Administrator permissions

- Test user: Intune TestUser

### Windows Endpoint

- Device name: INTUNE-AD-DC

- Operating system: Windows 11

- VMware virtual machine

- VMware model: VMware20,1

- Primary user: Intune TestUser

- Microsoft Entra joined: Yes

- Intune managed: Yes

---

## 1. Created an Intune Test User

Created a dedicated test account:

- Display name: Intune TestUser

- Job title: IT Support Test User

- Department: IT Support

- Employee type: Full-time

- Usage location: Nigeria

The user was assigned a Microsoft Intune Plan 1 trial license.

---

## 2. Windows 11 Microsoft Entra Join

During Windows 11 setup, the device was configured for:

> Set up for work or school

The Intune TestUser account was used to sign in.

The device successfully became Microsoft Entra joined.

Verification was performed using:

```cmd

dsregcmd /status

Important results included:
AzureAdJoined : YES
EnterpriseJoined : NO
DomainJoined : NO
DeviceAuthStatus : SUCCESS
TpmProtected : YES
This confirmed that the Windows 11 VM was successfully joined to Microsoft Entra ID.

3. Installed Microsoft Company Portal

The Microsoft Company Portal application was installed on the Windows 11 VM.

The Intune TestUser signed into Company Portal and completed the device setup process.

Company Portal reported:

You’re all set!

The device was then shown as:

Can access company resources

